  <content>
  <section><title>Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
<para>Let’s practice recognizing and implementing these strategies as we factor polynomials.</para>
<para>Remember, a polynomial is completely factored if, other than monomials, its factors are prime!</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">EXAMPLE 1</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(7x^3 − 21x^2 − 70x\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor out the GCF.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - In the parentheses, is it a binomial, a trinomial, or are there more than three terms?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - “Undo” FOIL.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely? </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">EXAMPLE 2</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(4x^2 + 8bx − 4ax − 8ab\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - There are four terms. Use grouping.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely?</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying. </para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">EXAMPLE 3</para>
<para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: \(40x^2y + 44xy − 24y\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Is there a GCF? </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor the trinomial with \(a \neq 1\) using the “\(ac\)” method or trial and error.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Is the expression factored completely? </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Check your answer by multiplying. </para>
<title>Try It: Implementing General Strategies for Factoring</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Find a strategy that fits and factor completely: </para>
    <para>\(27y^2 − 48\) </para>
    <para> \(6x^2 − 12xc + 6bx − 12bc\). </para>
    <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to factor these polynomials:</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> difference of squares; \(3(3y − 4)(3y + 4)\) </item>
  </list>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> grouping; \(6(x + b)(x − 2c)\)</item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
